A||||13||Black88GT||Jeep CJ7||||5||
Z||000000||Black88GT||01-26-2001||08:56 PM||||Anyone have anything bad to say about a CJ7 Jeep?  I have come to the grim reality that the Fiero is NOT a winter car, nor do I want it to be.  I have a good line on what appears to be a sweet deal for a CJ7, but I know nothing about Jeeps so I figured I would ask all you guys before I committed to a deal.<P><BR>TIA<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Hank is Here||01-26-2001||09:29 PM||||Jeff, <BR>What do you want to know?  I have five jeeps in total so have run into a lot.  But one thing<BR>JEEP<BR>Just<BR>Empty<BR>Every <BR>Pocket<P>I love my jeeps, great summer around town vehicles actually, not winter vehicles for me.  <P>I have a 5,6, and 7<BR>shoot away<P>Mark Johnson<P>------------------<BR>Hank is Here<BR>87 GT<BR>88 Coupe<BR>84 SE<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://a1060.g.akamai.net/f/1060/597/30minutes/www.zing.com/picture/p60e80970138058d8e3fae31692e151e0/fef43e85.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Hank is Here (edited 01-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||01-26-2001||09:35 PM||||Don't know 'bout CJ7's but our Cherokee has been pretty good.  We bought it used nearly 8 years ago with 80K+ miles.  Now it has 240K+ and still going.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||01-26-2001||10:03 PM||||How reliable are they?  What kind of engines did they have? What are some common rust spots or problem spots I should look for when "inspecting" one?<P>Oh yeah, what makes a new Wrangler better then the older CJ7's? <P><BR>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">               <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">               <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 01-26-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000004||2xsess||01-26-2001||10:17 PM||||In 1998 I believe they switched over to full coil-over suspension. <P>On the 7s you have to look at the inner wheel wells and the bed area for rust. A good idea is to take a magnet with you and stick it on the body around various areas to see if it sticks, if it doesn't then you know there's some bondo in there. But, if you're getting it real cheap then you can't complain too much. <BR>Most of the CJ7s that I've seen have a I-6 in them, not sure about the displacement though.<BR>Jeeps are like the Fieros, it's a bug. I still regret ever selling mine.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||01-26-2001||10:29 PM||||Our '88 Cherokee has a 4.0-liter inline six with 5-speed ZF ("Peugeot") manual transmission.  Engine is pretty solid; transmission is sweet; clutch slave cylinder design is <I>dumb</I>!  Slave cylinder is inside bellhousing; remove transmission to replace slave cylinder.<P>We have replaced 2 or 3 water pumps, power steering pump, crankshaft position sensor (common failing), manifold gasket, rear main seal, clutch, crankshaft damper, radiator, brakes, alternator, starter, and a half-dozen serpentine belts.  For a while, the engine had oil leak/consumption problems -- a combination of mediocre venting and a clogged catalytic converter.<P>We still like it, though.<P>I don't know how much of this applies to CJ7.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||BigBoyToys||01-26-2001||10:58 PM||||Check the rear frame area just above the rear shackles for rust through or fresh welds. Also the rear cross member just under the rear door/floor pan. Also always look at the cab mounts for rust and rust heading towards the cab mounts. Hardtops may have better cabs than the softops but both have the frames exposed. BBTs||||reg||1
Z||000007||SloPonti||01-27-2001||07:03 PM||||here is my picture of my 81 jeep cj7! my jeep has a 258 inline six and an automatic trans and factory air conditioning! in the summer i take the hardtop off and put on a bikini top and windjammer and duster! it is a great jeep-it runs not stop with no problems so far! i would not get rid of it even if i had to!!! i recommend getting a jeep but get a hardtop rather than a softop! they are great in the snow!!  i need to get custom plates for it so does anyone have any ideas for license plates?? <BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/boatr502/jeep2.jpg">  <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/boatr502/jeep3.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/boatr502/jeep3.jpg</A>  <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/boatr502/jeep1.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/boatr502/jeep1.jpg</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Hank is Here||01-27-2001||09:30 PM||||Okay, <BR>Well jeeps and reliability don't exactly mix.  A jeep that has large tires and has been modified are the ones to stay away from, they generally have some underlying problems.  <BR>Engines<BR>Stock were:<BR>150 AMC<BR>151 (yeah the Iron Duke from GM)<BR>232 (i forget if it was used in the 7 but common in postal jeeps)<BR>258<BR>304<BR>easy and common drop in's 360 and 401<BR>also people like to put a jeep 4.0L head on the 258 for a little more power.  <P>Look here for general mechanical info it is a good page <A HREF="http://www.off-road.com/jeep/tech/cj-7.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.off-road.com/jeep/tech/cj-7.htm</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Black88GT||01-27-2001||09:46 PM||||Interesting you say that Hank.  Here is the link to the one I am looking at.  What do you think?<BR> <A HREF="http://www.bmja.org/rigs/CJ/Longo.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.bmja.org/rigs/CJ/Longo.htm</A> <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||BN Boomer||01-27-2001||11:35 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/boomers_railroad_files/mvc-003f.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000011||Hank is Here||01-28-2001||12:12 AM||||It looks nice.  <BR>I would check it all over, check the diffs, tranny and xfer for water (milky look).  Look at the mods see if they are quality or home amde crap.  Some things seem cheepo like the napa lights.  Drive it and check for steering  wonder. Some people say jeeps are terriable on the highway, but my overgrown go-cart CJ5 handles fine cause I redid all of the steering sector.  <BR>If you are looking for more of a driver I say  go to a Wrangler, but if you want a play thing for off road and on-road go for the CJ.  Heck jeeps are the way I have learned my limited mechanical skills. So be open to learn and work on them.   ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||01-28-2001||06:43 AM||||I'd be leery of any vehicle that played extensively off road.  Lots of wear and tear.  From the pics and the mods, I'd guess that that Jeep has seen lots of off road miles.  Be careful.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Black88GT||01-28-2001||02:40 PM||||Yeah monkeyman, thats what I am afraid of.  I think I might just keep looking for a non-off road Jeep.  He did tell me it has some rust in the wheel wells.  I saw a '92 in the paper for 1000 bucks, bad tranny.  So who knows, my Jeep dreams might not be over yet.<P><BR>||||reg||1
